<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dakshinapatha</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dakshinapatha, the southern road, was the ancient Indian name for the trade route running from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges plain</a> south-west to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a>, and for the southern lands it reached. Mentioned in Buddhist texts, it linked cities such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shravasti">Shravasti</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ujjain">Ujjain</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paithan">Pratishthana</a> on the Godavari. The name later came to mean the Deccan as a whole, and the word Deccan derives from it.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dakshinapatha.jpg" alt="Dakshinapatha" width="320" height="226" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: George Tsiagalakis · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Periplous_of_the_Erythraean_Sea.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The name joins two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> words, dakshina, south, and patha, road. The later Vedic texts divide the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-subcontinent">Indian subcontinent</a> into three parts: Aryavarta in the north, Madhya Desha in the centre and Dakshinapatha in the south. The Aitareya Brahmana, of the first half of the first millennium BCE, names peoples of the south of Vindhyan and north <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a> origin, among them the Satvants, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vidarbha">Vidarbha</a>, Andhra, Nishadas and Kuntis. The grammarian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panini">Panini</a>, about 500 BCE, mentions the Asmaka Kingdom in connection with the south and with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalinga">Kalinga</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The term has carried three meanings. It can denote the ancient south of the subcontinent below <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uttarapatha">Uttarapatha</a>, usually the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a> and sometimes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamilakam">Tamilakam</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceylon">Ceylon</a> as well. It can mean the great southern highway, running from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magadha">Magadha</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paithan">Pratishthana</a>. It has also been used for a kingdom on the Godavari River. The Junagarh rock inscription of 150 CE, set up by an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indo-scythians">Indo-Scythian</a> king, records that he twice defeated Satakarni, the lord of Dakshinapatha, in fair fight and did not destroy him because of the nearness of their connection.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The southern road was one of two great highways that have linked the parts of the subcontinent since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a>. The other, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uttarapatha">Uttarapatha</a> or great northern road, ran from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taxila">Taxila</a> through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamuna">Yamuna</a> and followed that river south to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mathura">Mathura</a>; from there a route went on to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ujjain">Ujjain</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a> and to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bharuch">Broach</a> on the western coast. According to the writer Sanjeev Sanyal, the course of the northern road has stayed roughly the same since before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maurya-empire">Mauryas</a>, while the southern road appears to have drifted.</p><p>The route of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama">Rama</a> into exile in the epic may reflect an early version of it. By the time of the Buddha it started at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varanasi">Varanasi</a> and ran through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vidisha">Vidisha</a> in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paithan">Pratishthana</a>, now Paithan, and it probably extended to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Chola</a>, Chera and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandya-dynasty">Pandya</a> kingdoms of the far south. By the Mauryan period there would have been a branch from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ujjain">Ujjain</a> to the ports of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>, which made Ujjain a major city by the Gupta era. The modern highway that roughly corresponds to it runs much further east than the old road but still meets the northern road at Varanasi.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dakshinapatha” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Sutta Nipata tells of the Brahmin teacher Bavari, who lived on the Godavari in the Assaka country and sent his pupils north to meet the Buddha, which shows the kingdom's links along the southern trade route, the <b>Dakshinapatha</b>, to the Ganges valley.</blockquote><a href="/state/assaka">Assaka</a></li></ul></section>
